PR #2621  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Lack of formal normative rule tagging in the supervisor ISA spec (Sv39/48/57, Svnapot, Svpbmt, Svinval, Ssqosid) allows implementations to diverge on enforcing PTE reserved-bit traps, VA canonicality checks, TLB-invalidation fence ordering, PBMT aliasing coherence, and srmcfg access control, creating privilege-escalation paths through address aliasing, stale TLB entries, and cross-domain memory corruption.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:68-84  (macro definitions)
      SATP_MODE_39/48/57, SATP_PPN, SATP_ASID constants match the RISC-V privileged spec encodings: MODE values at bits 63-60 (0x8=Sv39, 0x9=Sv48, 0xa=Sv57), PPN mask 0x00000FFFFFFFFFFF, ASID at bits 44-59. Pure constants with no runtime behavior.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:330-330  (macro definitions)
      CSR_SATP (0x180) matches the privileged spec CSR address for supervisor address translation and protection. Pure constant, used correctly at all csr_write/csr_read sites.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:415-415  (macro definitions)
      CSR_MSTATUS (0x300) matches spec. Pure constant; all usage sites (entry.S, fpu.S, head.S, process.c) correctly read/write the machine status register with appropriate bit manipulations.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:378-380  (macro definitions)
      CSR_HENVCFG (0x60a) and CSR_HENVCFGH (0x61a) match the privileged spec. Used by KVM to configure guest-visible PBMTE/STCE/ADUE/CBIE/CBCFE/CBZE/PMM bits. The constant values are correct.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:420-421  (macro definitions)
      CSR_MENVCFG (0x30a) and MENVCFGH (0x31a) match spec. The access-control concern (henvcfg.ADUE read-only zero when menvcfg.ADUE=0) is a hardware enforcement property — the macro definitions themselves are correct. The risk lies in KVM's Svadu configuration (see vcpu.c analysis below).
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:234-243  (macro definitions)
      SMSTATEEN0 bit macros (AIA_IMSIC at bit 58, AIA at 59, AIA_ISEL at 60, HSENVCFG at 62, SSTATEEN0 at 63) match the Smstateen extension spec. KVM correctly sets SMSTATEEN0_HSENVCFG in hstateen0 to grant guest access to henvcfg.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/init.c:49-53  satp_mode
      satp_mode global initialised to SATP_MODE_57 (Sv57) as an optimistic default on 64-bit non-XIP kernels. This initial value is never used directly in a CSR write — set_satp_mode() is called first and downgrades satp_mode based on hardware probing before any CSR_SATP write occurs. Safe by construction.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/init.c:816-827  disable_pgtable_l5 / disable_pgtable_l4
      These functions downgrade satp_mode and update pgtable_l4_enabled/pgtable_l5_enabled flags. They modify in-memory state only — no CSR writes happen here. The actual satp transition occurs later in setup_vm_final() or set_satp_mode() retry path. The kernel_map.page_offset is also updated to match the new paging level.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/init.c:858-900  set_satp_mode
      Correctly probes hardware satp mode support: (1) limits satp_mode via cmdline and FDT 'mmu-type' property using min_not_zero(), preventing unsupported modes that could hang hardware; (2) builds identity-mapped page tables covering the function's own code; (3) writes CSR_SATP with identity mapping, reads back via csr_swap to check acceptance; (4) if mode not accepted, downgrades (Sv57→Sv48→Sv39) and retries. TLB flushed (local_flush_tlb_all()) both before and after CSR_SATP write. The probe-then-verify pattern is robust.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/init.c:1334-1370  setup_vm_final
      Builds swapper_pg_dir with fixmap, linear mapping, and kernel mappings, then activates via csr_write(CSR_SATP, PFN_DOWN(swapper_pg_dir) | satp_mode) followed by local_flush_tlb_all(). The satp_mode used is the validated mode from set_satp_mode(). Correct final activation sequence.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/context.c:192-194  set_mm_asid
      Atomic address-space switch: writes CSR_SATP with pgd PFN + ASID + validated satp_mode in a single CSR write. Conditionally flushes TLB afterward. The ASID is derived via cntx2asid() from the context allocator, and satp_mode is the globally validated mode. No TOCTOU window — single CSR write for both ASID and page-table switch.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/context.c:200-204  set_mm_noasid
