PR #2621  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Lack of formal normative rule tagging in the supervisor ISA spec (Sv39/48/57, Svnapot, Svpbmt, Svinval, Ssqosid) allows implementations to diverge on enforcing PTE reserved-bit traps, VA canonicality checks, TLB-invalidation fence ordering, PBMT aliasing coherence, and srmcfg access control, creating privilege-escalation paths through address aliasing, stale TLB entries, and cross-domain memory corruption.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-isa-manual/pr-2621/

  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:130-146  switch_to_next_domain_context
      SENVCFG swap (which controls PBMTE for Svpbmt translation) occurs AFTER the SFENCE.VMA at line 140. This means the TLB is flushed while the old domain's SENVCFG is still active, but the new domain's SATP is already loaded. Any speculative or interrupt-driven page-table walk between lines 140 and 144 would apply the old domain's PBMT semantics to the new domain's page tables. Additionally, no CBO.flush (clean/invalidate) instructions are issued during domain context switch. If two domains map the same physical page with conflicting Svpbmt memory attributes (e.g., one cacheable, one non-cacheable), stale dirty cache lines from the outgoing domain can be consumed by the incoming domain. The spec requires a fence + CBO.flush + fence sequence between mixed-cacheability accesses to the same physical page (Svpbmt_cacheable_aliasing_fence_flush_fence_required), but this is not enforced.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:145-146  switch_to_next_domain_context
      SRMCFG access is correctly guarded by sbi_hart_has_extension(SBI_HART_EXT_SSQOSID). The M-mode init path (sbi_hart.c:111-114) configures mstateen0.SRMCFG: set when Ssqosid is present, cleared otherwise. This prevents S-mode from accessing CSR SRMCFG (0x181) when Ssqosid is not implemented, satisfying the Ssqosid_srmcfg_access_illegal_instruction requirement. The domain context switch correctly saves/restores SRMCFG only on systems where it is architecturally present.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_tlb.c:80-99  sbi_tlb_local_sfence_vma
      Correctly issues SFENCE.VMA for TLB invalidation. The function handles both 'flush all' (start==0 && size==0) and page-range flush cases. The SFENCE.VMA instruction with VA operand provides architecturally stronger ordering than the Svinval SINVAL.VMA + SFENCE.W.INVAL + SFENCE.INVAL.IR sequence. On systems where S-mode uses Svinval directly, M-mode's SFENCE.VMA acts as a global fence that is architecturally ordered with respect to S-mode's SINVAL.VMA. The TLB sync protocol (tlb_sync counter + atomic operations) provides correct cross-hart handshaking: SFENCE.VMA on the target hart completes before the atomic_sub_return that signals the source hart, and the source hart spin-waits on the counter via atomic_read, satisfying the inter-hart TLB-invalidation fence ordering.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_ecall_legacy.c:91-114  sbi_ecall_legacy_handler
      Legacy SBI v0.1 REMOTE_SFENCE_VMA and REMOTE_SFENCE_VMA_ASID handlers correctly validate the S-mode hart mask pointer via sbi_load_hart_mask_unpriv() (which handles S-mode page faults by populating a trap_info for redirection). On valid hart mask, they delegate to sbi_tlb_request() which uses the IPI-based TLB sync protocol to execute SFENCE.VMA on all target harts. The start/size/asid parameters are read from the M-mode saved trap frame (regs->a1/a2/a3), which is immutable during handler execution, eliminating TOCTOU windows. Both 'flush all' (0,0) and page-range invalidation are correctly forwarded.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_tlb.c:211-227  tlb_entry_process
      The TLB sync protocol correctly sequences: (1) local SFENCE.VMA execution via tlb_entry_local_process(), then (2) atomic_sub_return on source harts' sync counters. The SFENCE.VMA instruction on the receiving hart provides intra-hart ordering (older stores ordered before younger loads/stores), and the atomic_sub_return (AMO with release semantics in RVWMO) provides inter-hart visibility. The source hart in tlb_sync() spin-waits via atomic_read until its counter reaches 0, ensuring it observes the target hart's TLB flush completion before proceeding. This satisfies the inter-hart fence ordering required by Svinval_sfence_w_inval_orders_before_sinval_vma.

